---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../styles/page-layouts.css';
import '../styles/story.css';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import freddyPhoto from '../assets/freddy-greensman.jpg';
import reel1 from '../assets/trouncetalks-reel-1.jpg';
import reel2 from '../assets/trouncetalks-reel-2.jpg';
import reel3 from '../assets/trouncetalks-reel-3.jpg';

const reels = [
  { image: reel1, href: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcO5xTcJfZ0/' },
  { image: reel2, href: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWHgmeo4eL/' },
  { image: reel3, href: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DavZ2WJIQX1/' },
];
---

<Layout
title="The Greensman — a story — Mike Trounce"
description="How two small fixes — cheaper hosting and a corrected flyer — became an ongoing tidy-up of everything The Greensman's customers see, from a quote video that tripled a job to a YouTube channel in the works."
>
<div class="cs-page">
<a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Mike Trounce</a>

<header class="cs-hero">
  <Image src={freddyPhoto} alt="Freddy Trounce of The Greensman working in a client's garden" width={1170} height={768} />
  <p class="cs-caption">Freddy Trounce, The Greensman — Elstead, Surrey</p>
</header>

<span class="cs-label">Story 1</span>
<h1>The Greensman</h1>
<p class="cs-meta">Garden &amp; grounds maintenance &middot; Ongoing</p>

<p class="cs-standfirst">
Freddy's business didn't need one big project. It needed someone to notice what was wrong — a website costing him over £100 a year to keep online, a flyer sending customers to the wrong address — and fix each thing quickly, before it cost him.
</p>

<div class="stats">
  <div class="stat">
    <span class="stat-num">&pound;100+</span>
    <span class="stat-label">saved every year, from next year, just by moving the site's hosting</span>
  </div>
  <div class="stat">
    <span class="stat-num">Minutes</span>
    <span class="stat-label">to fix the flyer — not hours, and not a redesign</span>
  </div>
  <div class="stat">
    <span class="stat-num">0</span>
    <span class="stat-label">edits needed for new Google reviews to appear on the site</span>
  </div>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>The website that cost &pound;125 a year to keep online</h2>
  <p>
  <a href="https://www.thegreensmanelstead.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">thegreensmanelstead.com</a> didn't need a redesign — it needed cheaper hosting. It sat on Wix's Light plan (&pound;108 a year) plus a domain (&pound;17 a year), set up by the original developer, likely as much in exchange for gardening work as for cash. Moving it to a self-hosted setup took very little time and carries no subscription — so from next year, that's &pound;100+ back in Freddy's pocket for no loss of website.
  </p>
  <p>
  While I was in there, I swapped the site's handful of hand-picked testimonials for an embedded feed of Freddy's Google Business reviews. New five-star reviews now appear on the site by themselves, as clients leave them on Google — without Freddy, or me, touching the code again.
  </p>
  <div class="compare">
    <div class="card before">
      <h3>Before</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Wix Light plan, &pound;108 a year</li>
        <li>Domain, &pound;17 a year</li>
        <li>Testimonials frozen at the last edit</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card after">
      <h3>After</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Self-hosted, no subscription</li>
        <li>Live Google review feed</li>
        <li>New reviews appear on their own</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
<details class="tech-detail" id="technical">
<summary>Technical detail: moving the domain off Wix</summary>
<p>
Wix locks nameserver editing for domains registered there, so pointing DNS at Cloudflare wasn't a case of changing a setting — the domain had to move to a registrar that allows nameserver changes first.
</p>
<p>
We transferred it to Namecheap, not Cloudflare's own registrar — Cloudflare Registrar only accepts domains that are already active on Cloudflare, and a domain can't go active on Cloudflare while its nameservers are still locked at Wix. Namecheap became the domain's new home; Cloudflare handles everything else.
</p>
<p>
Once the transfer to Namecheap completed, the domain's nameservers were pointed at Cloudflare's (<code>hal.ns.cloudflare.com</code> and <code>jill.ns.cloudflare.com</code>), which activated a free Cloudflare zone. The site itself runs on Cloudflare Pages, which issues a free SSL certificate and serves both the apex domain and <code>www</code> — the sort of setup that would normally sit behind a paid plan.
</p>
<p>
With the new setup live and verified, the Wix Light plan was cancelled — that's where the &pound;100+/year saving actually lands.
</p>
</details>
  <p>
  Moving the domain fixed the hosting bill, but it turned up a second, quieter problem: ask Google's AI Mode about The Greensman and it was still citing a dead Wix web address as the business's official site — a leftover from the old setup that doesn't even resolve any more. The real site had never actually been given the signals search engines use to trust a page as current.
  </p>
<details class="tech-detail" id="technical-seo">
<summary>Technical detail: giving Google something to go on</summary>
<p>
A <code>site:</code> search for thegreensmanelstead.com turned up zero indexed pages — Google had nothing current on file for the business at all, so its AI answers fell back on old cached data. The site itself had no schema.org markup, no canonical tag, no Open Graph tags, and no <code>robots.txt</code> or <code>sitemap.xml</code>.
</p>
<p>
Added LocalBusiness structured data (name, phone, service area, description — deliberately no street address, matching what the site already discloses publicly), a canonical tag, Open Graph tags, a <code>robots.txt</code>, and a <code>sitemap.xml</code>. Submitted the sitemap through Google Search Console and requested indexing directly — that only needed ownership of the domain's DNS, not Freddy's Google account, so it didn't have to wait on him.
</p>
<p>
The dead Wix address turned up in one more place: Freddy's Google Business Profile still listed it as the business's website. Fixed directly in Business Profile Manager, pointing it at thegreensmanelstead.com instead.
</p>
</details>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The flyer, fixed in minutes</h2>
  <p>
  Freddy already had a nice A5 flyer, designed on Canva by someone else and handed to him as a PDF to send straight to print. It did its job well, but had two problems: the website address on it was wrong, and it made no mention of the autumn leaf-clearing offer we wanted to push.
  </p>
  <p>
  I'd spotted the wrong URL simply by staying in regular contact with Freddy about how the business and the website were doing — that's the kind of thing that surfaces in conversation, not in a briefing document. The same conversations picked up that his review count had grown since the flyer was first designed, so the update brought more of those reviews onto the page too.
  </p>
  <ul class="fix-list">
    <li>Correct website address</li>
    <li>Autumn leaf-clearing offer, now on the page</li>
    <li>Up-to-date count of five-star reviews</li>
    <li>Nothing else touched — a design that worked, kept working</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
  The whole fix took minutes in Claude Design, reusing the design that already worked rather than starting again.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The drift underneath — ongoing</h2>
  <p>
  Fixing the flyer surfaced something bigger: the flyer and the website had been built by two different people who'd never spoken to each other. Different logo files, different type, small inconsistencies that add up to a business looking less joined-up than it actually is.
  </p>
  <p>
  That's now its own piece of work: bringing the two into line — the same logo, the same fonts (Caprasimo for headings, Figtree for body) — across the flyer and the rebuilt website, so everything Freddy hands a customer looks like it comes from the same place.
  </p>
  <div class="drift">
    <span class="chip">Flyer — Canva, builder one</span>
    <span class="arrow">+</span>
    <span class="chip">Website — Wix, builder two</span>
    <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <span class="chip to">One brand, same logo and type</span>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The upsell reel</h2>
  <p>
  Freddy went to quote an overgrown garden on a student let. I went along to take photos and some video, the way I've started doing on a few of his jobs. The front hedge was well above head height; round the back, what had been a lawn was mostly bramble. Freddy's original quote only covered the front.
  </p>
  <p>
  Rather than leave it as a spoken quote and a follow-up text, I cut the footage into a short vertical video the same day — built in Claude Code, the same tool behind this site — and sent it along with the written quote.
  </p>
  <div class="reel-embed">
    <video controls preload="metadata" poster="/media/kabir-poster.jpg" playsinline>
      <source src="/media/kabir-reel.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
    </video>
  </div>
  <p class="cs-caption">The reel sent with the quote — shot and cut the same day.</p>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-num">&pound;100 &rarr; &pound;300</span>
      <span class="stat-label">the quote, before and after</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-num">3&times;</span>
      <span class="stat-label">the size of the job Freddy walked in to quote</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-num">Same visit</span>
      <span class="stat-label">no second call, no chasing — agreed on the spot</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
  Seeing the whole garden laid out in the video, not just the hedge he'd come to quote, made the case for covering the back as well as the front. The client agreed to both before Freddy had left the drive: the &pound;100 hedge job became a &pound;300 clearance of the whole garden. One small job, not a trend line — but a concrete example of the thing I keep telling founders: the extra bit of effort, a video instead of a sentence, is often where the extra revenue actually sits.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The missed call, and the WhatsApp pile-up</h2>
  <p>
  Freddy missed a call with me. When I got him on the phone after, he said it straight: he's getting overwhelmed by WhatsApp. Every lead, every quote, every customer chasing a date — it all lands in the same stream, mixed in with everything else, and it's the one place he can't afford to just switch off. "It's where everybody does business nowadays," he said.
  </p>
  <p>
  That's the same habit that caught the wrong URL on the flyer: being in regular enough contact that a founder tells you the thing that's actually wearing him down, not just the thing he thinks you want to hear. This one wasn't a website problem or a design problem — it was that there was no way to see what in a long chat list actually needed him today versus what could wait.
  </p>
  <p>
  So that morning, I built a connector between Claude and WhatsApp — running locally, nothing in the cloud — and proved it end to end: reading chats, sorting what needs a reply today from what's waiting on someone else, pulling the job details out of a lead conversation so it doesn't just sit in the thread. Same tool, same habit as the quote reel — notice the real problem on the spot, then go and fix it, the same day.
  </p>
  <ul class="fix-list">
    <li>Reads and triages a WhatsApp chat list on request — what needs a reply today, what's waiting on someone else, what can be ignored</li>
    <li>Drafts replies for review — nothing sends without a confirm</li>
    <li>Pulls a lead's job, quote status and last message out of the chat it's buried in</li>
    <li>Can log leads straight into HubSpot, so there's one list instead of a dozen chats</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
  It's proven on my own WhatsApp so far. The next step is setting up the same connector on Freddy's side, so he gets the triage directly instead of routing it through me.
  </p>
<details class="tech-detail" id="technical-whatsapp">
<summary>Technical detail: how the WhatsApp connector works</summary>
<p>
It runs through Local MCP on a Mac that's left on and connected — WhatsApp has no official API for this, so the link uses an unofficial client (Wacli), paired the same way WhatsApp Web is: scan a QR code once from the phone, no password involved.
</p>
<p>
What it can do today: read a chat's recent messages on request and summarise them, draft a reply for a human to approve before anything sends, and write structured data (like a lead's details) out to another system such as HubSpot.
</p>
<p>
What it can't do yet: watch WhatsApp in the background and alert on new messages — it only looks when asked, so the Mac has to be on and someone has to prompt it. It also can't listen to voice notes or read images yet, which matters for Freddy specifically, since a lot of what he gets sent is audio. And because the client is unofficial, there's a small standing risk of WhatsApp restricting the account, which is worth knowing before leaning on it for anything business-critical.
</p>
</details>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Freddy's own following</h2>
  <p>
  Freddy posts the day-to-day of building the business himself, as <a href="https://www.instagram.com/trouncetalks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@trouncetalks</a> on Instagram — unpolished, first-person, and hard for a competitor to fake.
  </p>
  <div class="social-grid">
    {reels.map((reel) => (
      <a class="reel-card" href={reel.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <Image src={reel.image} alt="Reel from @trouncetalks on Instagram" width={360} height={640} />
        <span class="reel-play" aria-hidden="true">&#9654;</span>
        <span class="reel-label">View on Instagram</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Next: The Greensman on YouTube</h2>
  <p>
  Instagram Reels are Freddy's personal story — first-person, unpolished, about him — and they reach people roughly his own age. A good number of his actual and potential clients are older, don't use Instagram much, but do use YouTube, and some of them are exactly the people who hire a gardener.
  </p>
  <p>
  Rather than split his personal account in two, the plan is a second channel on a second platform, with a different subject: not Freddy's journey, but The Greensman's work — built from the same habit of photos and video on site that already feeds his Instagram, just cut and framed around the business instead of around him.
  </p>
  <p>
  The channel doesn't exist yet. It'll sit under Freddy's own Google account, so the business owns it outright, with the first few pieces of footage already gathered on site ready to go up once it's set up.
  </p>
</section>

<p class="pullquote">
None of this was one big project. It's what happens when someone keeps a proper eye on a small business's moving parts — a website, a flyer, a growing pile of reviews — and fixes what's actually wrong, quickly, rather than waiting for a bigger brief.
</p>

<p class="cs-foot">
<a href="/">&larr; Mike Trounce</a>
</p>
</div>
</Layout>

<style>
.stats {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  gap: 12px;
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}
.stat {
  background: var(--bg-raised);
  border-radius: 16px;
  padding: 1.1rem 1rem;
  box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(var(--shadow) / 0.05), 0 12px 24px -16px rgba(var(--shadow) / 0.35);
}
.stat-num {
  display: block;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--illus-a);
  margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
}
.stat-label {
  font-size: 0.85rem;
  color: var(--ink-soft);
  line-height: 1.45;
}
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}

.compare {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: 12px;
  margin-top: 1.25rem;
}
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .compare { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}
.card {
  border-radius: 16px;
  padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
  background: var(--bg-raised);
}
.card.before { border: 1px solid var(--line); }
.card.after { border: 1px solid var(--illus-a); }
.card h3 {
  margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}
.card.after h3 { color: var(--illus-a); }
.card ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding-left: 1.1rem;
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}
.card li + li { margin-top: 0.3rem; }

.fix-list {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  padding: 0;
}
.fix-list li {
  color: var(--ink-soft);
  padding-left: 1.6rem;
  position: relative;
}
.fix-list li + li { margin-top: 0.35rem; }
.fix-list li::before {
  content: '\2713';
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  color: var(--illus-a);
  font-weight: 700;
}

.drift {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.6rem;
  margin-top: 1.25rem;
}
.chip {
  background: var(--illus-b);
  border-radius: 999px;
  padding: 0.45rem 0.9rem;
  font-size: 0.85rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}
.chip.to {
  background: var(--illus-a);
  color: #fff;
}
.arrow { color: var(--ink-faint); font-weight: 600; }

.social-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  gap: 12px;
  margin-top: 1.25rem;
}
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .social-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 8px; }
}

.reel-card {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  aspect-ratio: 9 / 16;
  border-radius: 16px;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--bg-raised);
  box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(var(--shadow) / 0.05), 0 12px 24px -16px rgba(var(--shadow) / 0.35);
}
.reel-card img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  display: block;
}
.reel-play {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  width: 2.4rem;
  height: 2.4rem;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  padding-left: 0.15rem;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: rgba(46, 44, 34, 0.45);
  color: #fff;
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  backdrop-filter: blur(2px);
}
.reel-label {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  padding: 0.6rem 0.5rem 0.5rem;
  font-size: 0.72rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-align: center;
  color: #fff;
  background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(46, 44, 34, 0.75), rgba(46, 44, 34, 0));
}
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .reel-label { font-size: 0.62rem; padding: 0.5rem 0.3rem 0.4rem; }
  .reel-play { width: 2rem; height: 2rem; font-size: 0.75rem; }
}

.reel-embed {
  max-width: 320px;
  margin: 1.5rem auto 0;
  border-radius: 20px;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--bg-raised);
  box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(var(--shadow) / 0.05), 0 18px 34px -18px rgba(var(--shadow) / 0.4);
}
.reel-embed video {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 9 / 16;
  object-fit: cover;
  background: #000;
}
.reel-embed + .cs-caption {
  text-align: center;
  margin-top: 0.7rem;
}

.pullquote {
  margin: 3rem 0 0;
  padding: 1.4rem 1.6rem;
  background: var(--illus-b);
  border-radius: 20px;
  font-size: 1.1rem;
  line-height: 1.55;
  color: var(--ink);
}
</style>
